speechtext:
Mr. Chairman yesterday evening there was some talk of Garfield MacKinnon. There were interesting exchanges from both sides. Since I myself brought this question up on April 14, I would like to add a few words to complete the discussion. On April 28 the hon. member for St. Johns-Iberville (Mr. Rheaume) brought
the matter up again; he was told by the hon. Postmaster General that Garfield MacKinnon has been going around the country for the last twelve years. Mr. Chairman, if Garfield MacKinnon has been exploiting our letter earners for twelve years it is no fault of mine for up to last January I had never even heard of the man. The first time I knew of his existence was when he came to Plessisville;
I immediately warned our mail carriers not to join this association and told them I would find out about this man as soon as I got to Ottawa. When I had obtained the information I wanted I gave it to the house;
I proved that this man is really a highway robber who has been mulcting people for the past twelve years.
The hon. Postmaster General stated on the floor of the house that he had given a satisfactory explanation to the member for Megantic, after the necessary inquiries bad been made. I can say that the Postmaster General, the deputy minister, the superintendent and all his colleagues helped me in my efforts to obtain an investigation. But the minister added that he could do nothing because his legal adviser had informed me that the affidavit I had given him was not sufficient to warrant a conviction. At the time this explanation satisfied me; but since then I have consulted competent lawyers, and the hon. members for Drummond-Arthabaska (Mr. Girouard) and for St. Hyacinthe-Rouville (Mr. Fontaine) have told me the affidavit sufficed to bring about this man's arrest.
